{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Facilitator Ron Piccolo gave a brief overview of today's agenda and explained his role in the discussion. Darren Gray, County Manager, updated the Board on some of the backup materials included in today's agenda. He discussed the Key Strategic Priorities (KSPs) handout (included in the agenda backup) and noted the KSPs won't be reviewed in detail, but if the Board has any questions regarding them, staff is available.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 David Johnson, Property Appraiser, provided a detailed Property Valuation Update as included in the agenda backup. He advised he is not going to review the "HJR 203-Elimination of Non-School Property Tax for Homesteads" and "Estimated Impact in \par
Seminole of HJR203" slides because they are no longer relevant.\par
\par
There were few Board comments and questions.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Tim Jecks, Office of Management and Budget Director, reviewed the Budget Work Session presentation as included in the agenda backup.\par
\par
Mr. Gray discussed the increase in law enforcement budgets. He explained how an MSTU could be used to create a dedicated funding source to the Sheriff so that the budgets don't increase as much as they have. However, from his experience, those MSTU's don't work, and they would still be looking to make up whatever that difference would be. He recommends they look at the whole budget with the Constitutionals, and then figure out their priorities and how they want to fund them. Commissioner Constantine stated the Board saw those dramatic increases and had to keep their budgets balanced. Mr. Gray advised staff has asked the Constituionals to keep their increase at a relatively low percentage. Mr. Jecks added staff doesn't have a commitment to a percentage, but everyone understands the situation they're in. Commissioner Constantine stated the County cannot sustain their level of services with the increase the Constitutionals are bringing. Commissioner Zembower explained why he is not interested in pursing the Sheriff MSTU. Vice Chairman Lockhart stated when you look at how it has to be portioned out with the incorporated vs. the unincorporated, it is very unpalatable. It is important to keep the conversation going on how important it is that the Sheriff and Fire provide services within the cities. She'd like to put the conversation on ice right now until they have more people willing to participate, recognizing they have skin in the game. Commissioner Dallari noted he supports the views of Commissioner Zembower and Vice Chairman Lockhart. Chairman Herr advised she agrees they should leave that alone for now, and they made a lot of progress from last year. Regarding the Use of Property Taxes slide, Mr. Gray noted this is one of Mr. Jecks' best slides because it really speaks to what the issue is and what the challenges are. Discussion ensued regarding gas tax, transportation, regional collaborative entities, and SunRail planning.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mr. Gray provided opening comments explaining the importance of this discussion. Daniel Entwistle, Curium US Director, began the presentation as included in the agenda backup. His team Charlotte Metcalf, PHR, Senior Consultant, and Justin Ward, Senior Consultant, reviewed some of the slides in the presentation as well.\par
\par
There was Board discussion including suggestions and questions. Chairman Herr advised she believes AI is to assist staff, not to replace. Commissioner Zembower stated the reality is costs of operating government continue to escalate, and this is one thing that could help that cost from a productivity standpoint. Commissioner Dallari and Vice Chairman Lockhart showed their support for the idea. Mr. Entwistle explained they are planning on an ambassador group, which is a controlled test group to learn and pilot a program in a controlled environment. They'll also start to see more of the shadow AI. The role of the working party is to support with the governance, and then there will be a training aspect as well. It's something that is front of the mind of the people they've already worked with in the County. He talked about how they would roll-out a policy and get people to adhere to it. Mr. Gray reminded that they have not contracted with Curium yet. Commissioner Zembower stated this is in the very early stages of the discussion, and there are certainly still a lot of questions. Vice Chairman Lockhart confirmed that the County Attorney's Office is already working on a policy. County Attorney Kate Latorre advised there is a draft policy in place, but it hasn't been approved by staff or the Board yet. Mr. Gray added the State has a government policy coming out as well.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Steve Koontz, Assistant County Manager, reviewed the Building Cultural Excellence presentation as included in the agenda backup. There was little Board discussion. Mr. Piccolo submitted a report on Artificial Intelligence in Local Governance (received and filed). Mr. Gray discussed the County's Organizational Chart and reviewed the recent staff changes. He stated they are doing succession planning so they don't have vacancies and are setting up employees so they can step-up to fill those vacancies. They want to make sure they have a plan and aren't just sitting stagnant. Chairman Herr advised their cultural perspective has shifted the way they operate. It used to be very linear, but now they're seeing policies being drafted in anticipation of something moving forward. That is a huge cultural shift. There's also a shift around public messaging; that used to be disconnected, but they're not doing that anymore which makes it much easier for them to be public messengers. \par
\par
The Chairman recessed the meeting at 11:35 a.m., reconvening at 12:47 p.m.\par
\par
Mr. Gray advised how the afternoon items will be presented, noting Mr. Jecks will present the financial aspect of each item after it's been presented. He would like questions asked as they go through the presentations because staff does need direction, but more questions may come up after Mr. Jecks discusses the financials.\par
}

