{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Tawny Olore, Public Works Director, addressed the Board and provided the department's budget presentation as included in the agenda backup. In regard to Scout, Commissioner Lockhart requested staff figure out a way of communicating that Scout is moving more people from more areas to more areas than would have had access to transportation before, and that they are still saving money. She discussed the Lynx funding formula model change: what they were paying, what they're paying now, and why they're paying more now. She would like to be able to easily communicate that story to the public. Mr. Gray advised staff will include all of that information at the second BCC meeting of July. There will be a full Scout briefing with the Board and what the experience has been, including the Lynx information, and wait times. Regarding the $150 per capita approach, Commissioner Dallari stated staff needs to look at how that relates to surrounding counties and do that comparison so that they can show the public that they are trying to cut the budget and be more efficient. Chairman Herr added it has to be an apples-to-apples comparison, and Vice Chairman Lockhart added the comparisons should be Counties that have access to SunRail. Chairman Herr stated in case it ever gets used external to the presentation without all the verbiage that was added today, the slide on page 241, "Traffic Movement," needs to include a footer of what was not included in that or it will become "you're only doing this," and she would like to prevent that.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 John Slot, Director of Organizational Performance & Strategy, addressed the Board and provided the department's budget presentation as included in the agenda backup. Commissioner Zembower stated regarding working with the Fire Department on signal preemption, they're saving fuel and wear-and-tear on the vehicles; but there's also a pollution aspect on that as well, so there should be a byproduct to emphasize the environmental benefits. Mr. Gray stated what gets measured is what gets done, and staff is on top of the Seminal Moments program daily. He moved the administration of Seminal Moments into the Strategy Office because they needed someone to coordinate the program and work with each of the directors. The directors are working great with each other and their teams because now it's drilling down to the people who actually know how to get the savings. There's also the newly launched AI Ambassador Program with folks across the organization, at different levels, coming together to implement the program and how it's going to affect the County's performance for residents and business owners. Staff is on top of it every day looking at the best way to deliver services at the lowest costs.\par
}

