{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Shannon Wetzel, Watershed Management Division Manager, reported on the increase of locally acquired dengue cases. The Florida Department of Health has confirmed two locally acquired cases in the county and issued a health alert. She explained mosquito control operates border to border in the county, which includes going into the cities. They have seen an increase in mosquito activity to continue, and they continue to treat countywide. In addition to conducting normal routine mosquito control treatment, they are conducting enhanced treatment within those disease vector areas. That includes increased nighttime fogging, daytime larva sighting, and also canvassing those disease vector areas to treat and target standing water areas which are breeding mosquitoes. She reminded mosquito control staff cannot access all of the areas that are potentially breeding mosquitoes. The mosquitoes that contain dengue are specific to container breeders, which are typically found in people's backyards. Mosquitoes can start breeding in standing water that's there for three to five days. They encourage residents to protect themselves from dengue by monitoring and dumping out containers that can hold water. She recommended wearing protective clothing and using mosquito repellant if going outside. Upon Vice Chairman Lockhart's inquiry, Ms. Wetzel advised residents can use mosquito dunks, which is a larvicide bacteria that prevents larva from emerging and can be bought at hardware stores and retailers, to use in their yards, fountains, plants like bromeliads that hold water, etc.\par
}

